<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Wuhuan</h1><p class="st-meta">206 BCE – 207 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Nomads of the north-eastern frontier who served the Han as border guards and cavalry until Cao Cao crushed them at White Wolf Mountain in 207.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Like the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei</a>, the Wuhuan were survivors of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/donghu">Donghu</a>, who took refuge at the Wuhuan mountains after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modu-chanyu">Modu</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> destroyed them around 206 BCE. They lived in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoning">Liaoning</a> and paid tribute to the Xiongnu.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After the Han general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huo-qubing">Huo Qubing</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> in 119 BCE, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a> resettled Wuhuan bands along its frontier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> as guards and set up a Protector of the Wuhuan. They were herders and hunters ruled by elected chiefs, without a single king, and Wuhuan cavalry became famous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> in Han wars.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the late 2nd century Wuhuan chiefs such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tadun">Tadun</a> allied with the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shao">Yuan Shao</a>. In 207 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> crossed the mountains and killed Tadun at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-white-wolf-mountain">White Wolf Mountain</a>, then moved the Wuhuan into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, where their horsemen served his armies and they gradually merged with the Chinese and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/wuhuan.jpg" alt="Wuhuan" width="320" height="208"><figcaption>Image: User Yuninjie on en.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Han_map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>206 BCE – 207 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tadun</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Steppe shamanism</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a>, <a href="/countries/mongolia">Mongolia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wuhuan confederation</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=0&amp;focus=wuhuan" aria-label="Wuhuan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7378 1013 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Wuhuan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7444 753h0M7769 1191h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7698 1269h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7698 1269h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day China. <a href="/atlas?year=0&amp;focus=wuhuan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
